#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161718 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dd14a52-8c49-563a-85b2-8d3e4a3f4cff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/id #uuid "4aae1963-085e-5d98-a22e-5d475f709769" :question/math-academy-id "e-8184" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms are given in their canonical forms?\n\n- $P(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-2x_2x_3+x_3^2$\n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x},$ where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/assets/d041c39fe285ee5fdbd8022b70a8043a4ff07cbeb1dae8c49905248df1b86d01.png)\n\n- $R(\\mathbf{x})=x_3^2-2x_1^2$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms ($x_i x_j$ where $i \\neq j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(\\mathbf{x})$ is *not* in canonical form since it contains the cross-product term $2x_2x_3.$\n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is in canonical form. Indeed, its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\n- $R(\\mathbf{x})$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q(\\mathbf{x})$ and $R(\\mathbf{x})$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161719 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c4037e5-607d-5cc4-9ce8-f7f1cd2c5aae" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 2D-Case"}] :question/id #uuid "e0b3629c-0f62-56ef-9e7e-f811958bf36d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8185" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=9x_1^2-18x_1x_2+5x_2^2$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/assets/cc77cb76fcab6bd72211e7e66d5fee5ba87c0ba1465571aa88baf26beb196a22.png)\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group $x_1^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_1$ and factor out the coefficient of $x_1^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =9{x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} & =9([{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}]-{x}_{2}^{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} & =9({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/assets/e1048614ed3dda333597bf32339f23a1585308e630233e3d11751c48c597ed52.png)\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ $4 \\quad$ and $\\quad x_1-x_2 .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161720 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e5e149-54cd-528e-b50e-1cafecb7b77a" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 3D-Case"}] :question/id #uuid "f889b145-edc7-5721-ab60-c3395b86e4b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8186" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-4x_3^2+2x_1x_2-4x_1x_3+2x_2x_3$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/assets/bf0297b0d758d02198d01565050bc5f3b02b0964336d3b84fdca331fabed6051.png)\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group $x_1^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_1$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3})+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}))+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})+({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2})+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-8{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group $x_2^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_2,$ factor out the coefficient of $x_2^2,$ and complete the square inside the second parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{2}{x}_{3})-8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-([{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+9{x}_{3}^{2}]-9{x}_{3}^{2})-8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-3{x}_{3})^{2}+9{x}_{3}^{2}-8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-3{x}_{3})^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/assets/84f88cbb41e573d7ed0793919e0ad25477a46637d09144ca9f462e12f0bc6e90.png)\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x_1+x_2-2x_3 \\quad$ and $\\quad x_2-3x_3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dd14a52-8c49-563a-85b2-8d3e4a3f4cff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be9417a3-026f-5fa7-bb97-ab92fc702019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379021} {:answer/id #uuid "a92d617a-4b4e-5add-b0b5-b948f38ab4ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379022} {:answer/id #uuid "6565f6f3-11fb-5380-b898-1fb6f86a80f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379023} {:answer/id #uuid "3f183202-7067-50a8-b0e4-55e7cd79b36f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q(x)$ and $R(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379024} {:answer/id #uuid "6b2d48f7-288b-5a3a-b822-317c59a9d1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be9417a3-026f-5fa7-bb97-ab92fc702019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379021} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4985610-d461-59e5-89dd-ea940d6b0a2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36a5116d-e670-58dd-8a1a-da9cc3b98bc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-140982" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms are given in their canonical forms?\n\n- $P(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$\n- $Q(x)=({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2}$\n- $R(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax,$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms (${x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ where $i\\ne j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(x)$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\n- $Q(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since \n\n$$\nQ(x)=({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2}={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\n So,  it contains the cross-product term $2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$\n\n- $R(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since its corresponding matrix is not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P(x)$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e5e149-54cd-528e-b50e-1cafecb7b77a" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 3D-Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0943c76c-697f-56af-ab7d-4420b2d56bd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379028} {:answer/id #uuid "f402b1bc-56fd-53e5-9bd5-d14f3dd16c1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379029} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a159c1-f105-5c10-8585-d20a73aa7d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379030} {:answer/id #uuid "86c628e7-32d8-56e1-be95-f3f3ec9538fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379031} {:answer/id #uuid "18a67707-3e0a-5dd0-a142-fdebc7fccd12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2a159c1-f105-5c10-8585-d20a73aa7d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379030} :answer-field/id #uuid "15d4c976-bc0f-5630-9d2b-0b1c3a1fa5a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c82f5129-4cd7-5326-9dc3-84b2ccee4d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140986" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+40{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+8{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+40{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{3})-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+40{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}))-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+40{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-6{x}_{3})+({x}_{2}-6{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-6{x}_{3})^{2})-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+40{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-6{x}_{3})^{2}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+40{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+12{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-36{x}_{3}^{2}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+40{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group ${x}_{2}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{2},$ factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{2}^{2},$ and complete the square inside the second parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3})+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-([{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{3}^{2}]-4{x}_{3}^{2})+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}+8{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+8{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dd14a52-8c49-563a-85b2-8d3e4a3f4cff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "927950b9-777b-52fe-a030-bfeeeab2fadf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379035} {:answer/id #uuid "68d82d57-6d11-5c0f-97f6-6bef96d980cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379036} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8bd3e0-07d2-510c-971f-b9e7c4824ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379037} {:answer/id #uuid "4ede2530-156c-54e5-b1a8-94d603b144c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q(x)$ and $R(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379038} {:answer/id #uuid "40a4af37-805d-55e3-9cc2-b471110cdd90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b8bd3e0-07d2-510c-971f-b9e7c4824ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379037} :answer-field/id #uuid "f272fdf0-5d79-51da-a4c5-15c433e9fdb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63d0ad07-b447-57fc-bb77-4bbdba118ac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94125" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms are given in their canonical forms?\n\n- $P(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}$\n- $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}$\n- $R(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax,$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms (${x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ where $i\\ne j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since it contains the cross-product term $2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}.$\n\n- $Q(x)$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\n- $R(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since its corresponding matrix is *not* diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q(x)$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dd14a52-8c49-563a-85b2-8d3e4a3f4cff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185ed8f4-fd06-593d-98bb-202de626a977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379042} {:answer/id #uuid "b96f588b-dc25-570c-ba14-1c51cb2a25e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379043} {:answer/id #uuid "2b351129-799e-5010-84b0-b56ab77e660a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P(x)$ and $R(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379044} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9952bd-add3-5932-991d-a5b40f2e8c75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379045} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb10cf8-7261-508e-ae05-771e882b1f73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q(x)$ and $R(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b96f588b-dc25-570c-ba14-1c51cb2a25e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379043} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d4a88bd-9463-535e-8b04-97d89b49c30f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bcaa271-3d5a-59dc-bfa8-3f9708c45d5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94127" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms are given in their canonical forms?\n\n- $P(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$\n- $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax,$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{3}$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms (${x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ where $i\\ne j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(x)$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\n- $Q(x)$ is in canonical form. Indeed, its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\n- $R(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since it contains the cross-product term ${x}_{1}{x}_{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dd14a52-8c49-563a-85b2-8d3e4a3f4cff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb5b2930-88c5-5cce-9c8f-07359ccd4db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379049} {:answer/id #uuid "aa413377-363a-5358-b6fb-84fb2523b3ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379050} {:answer/id #uuid "158c9ef9-2f2c-5ec9-a8c5-cebc53d7532d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P(x)$ and $R(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379051} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ad7033-0797-5fb5-b1ab-357a8ff3ca68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379052} {:answer/id #uuid "1f991554-fc3e-53e2-8aa1-ccc26f696659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb5b2930-88c5-5cce-9c8f-07359ccd4db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379049} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c5fe962-adbf-5f6c-a85a-09731a4e9412" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac19d765-c6e4-5363-aff8-6d46d36905da" :question/math-academy-id "q-94132" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms are given in their canonical forms?\n\n- $P(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}$\n- $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax,$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\n- $R(x)=2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms (${x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ where $i\\ne j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(x)$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\n- $Q(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since its corresponding matrix is not diagonal.\n\n- $R(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since it contains the cross-product term $2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P(x)$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dd14a52-8c49-563a-85b2-8d3e4a3f4cff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f1e28ab-e1c9-5165-b1ac-93f273373e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379056} {:answer/id #uuid "49359dd7-70d9-5e3c-9b33-ae6be2c2d4e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379057} {:answer/id #uuid "bade8cc7-23b3-58f4-b731-5f8de10dcb50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379058} {:answer/id #uuid "71b3af53-6994-50b9-976c-46b8f4831743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q(x)$ and $R(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379059} {:answer/id #uuid "56f01c52-66bc-545e-bb1e-b56d0667e187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bade8cc7-23b3-58f4-b731-5f8de10dcb50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379058} :answer-field/id #uuid "566e5a81-eefa-5ae8-899f-caa5a7284b2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4e32ab9-8f46-5714-bca5-d7cb14182740" :question/math-academy-id "q-94135" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms are given in their canonical forms?\n\n- $P(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax,$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\n- $Q(x)={x}_{3}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2}$\n- $R(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{3}-3{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms (${x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ where $i\\ne j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since its corresponding matrix is not diagonal.\n\n- $Q(x)$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\n- $R(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since it contains the cross-product term ${x}_{1}{x}_{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q(x)$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c4037e5-607d-5cc4-9ce8-f7f1cd2c5aae" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 2D-Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5afdfeec-a314-50e6-a60e-783eb6ac0929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and ${x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379063} {:answer/id #uuid "292cce09-6793-50b8-bc54-dab5a48c3014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379064} {:answer/id #uuid "55ed7227-a241-5096-9dc6-a193393aee0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379065} {:answer/id #uuid "0f2d282d-70cb-5802-a891-07cce3420428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379066} {:answer/id #uuid "56b9fab5-cff2-5750-a0cc-2f3e3254d0da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f2d282d-70cb-5802-a891-07cce3420428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379066} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9dba355-9e75-5ab3-af62-8644c7fe96fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "114299ac-5cb6-5ace-82c7-9724e64c9935" :question/math-academy-id "q-94138" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,{y}_{1}^{2}-5{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{1}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(4{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2})-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})-{x}_{2}^{2} & =4([{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}]-{x}_{2}^{2})-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4(({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})-{x}_{2}^{2} & =4({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n4{y}_{1}^{2}-5{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ $4\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c4037e5-607d-5cc4-9ce8-f7f1cd2c5aae" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 2D-Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a61c089e-855c-5bc9-b034-517a3d1b718f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379070} {:answer/id #uuid "3bff65ef-68b3-591a-9cc4-fea0df0d6343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379071} {:answer/id #uuid "96d30665-c1f2-5163-b85d-34307ec4254a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and ${x}_{2}-{x}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379072} {:answer/id #uuid "a21ae77c-2ca6-515f-80ab-946786a909b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379073} {:answer/id #uuid "90319618-999c-56ff-95f5-70bbc008f51a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a61c089e-855c-5bc9-b034-517a3d1b718f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379070} :answer-field/id #uuid "60347550-5557-5b74-b1fd-70e810ae5ac1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "106b083d-c143-5f48-8526-3cd804d072e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94139" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)=9{x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,{y}_{1}^{2}+7{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{1}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =9{x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+16{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+16{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+16{x}_{2}^{2} & =9([{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}]-{x}_{2}^{2})+16{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})+16{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})+16{x}_{2}^{2} & =9({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}+7{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n9{y}_{1}^{2}+7{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ $9\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c4037e5-607d-5cc4-9ce8-f7f1cd2c5aae" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 2D-Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b707a638-f8ba-5379-80ae-80d18efc4ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $x_{1} + 3x_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160909} {:answer/id #uuid "d316df5f-eb56-540a-90b2-a38e88608f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $x_{1} + x_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160910} {:answer/id #uuid "33338ce3-eac4-5358-ac52-9630cd86cd9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $x_{1} + 2x_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160911} {:answer/id #uuid "527b2681-2d12-58c6-a1a8-faebae067e03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $x_{1} - 3x_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160912} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4ba19c-3574-561f-9b4c-19b852cb529a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $x_{1} - 2x_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b707a638-f8ba-5379-80ae-80d18efc4ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $x_{1} + 3x_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160909} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bcfe45b-dbad-5a1c-86a5-77078d6e137b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca81ca2d-2a11-5281-bdfb-66b5a7ca3f2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94142" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+29{x}_{2}^{2}+18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{1}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+29{x}_{2}^{2}+18{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =(3{x}_{1}^{2}+18{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} & =3([{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}]-9{x}_{2}^{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3(({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} & =3({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}-27{x}_{2}^{2}+29{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ $3\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c4037e5-607d-5cc4-9ce8-f7f1cd2c5aae" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 2D-Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a5579d2-55b9-50e5-8fde-8384a84cbe16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and ${x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379077} {:answer/id #uuid "854e487c-4182-5f10-b727-ee195380e695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379078} {:answer/id #uuid "3cea3095-4069-5aca-a04b-ac1ee4ec0caa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379079} {:answer/id #uuid "120dc73c-da7e-50d0-b3cb-4b9dbf033862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and ${x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379080} {:answer/id #uuid "4a742eab-a8f5-5273-a63b-7b0fcb8c396d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "854e487c-4182-5f10-b727-ee195380e695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379078} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1568567-5c21-55e2-949c-92784f07ef65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "403b4989-6bac-5fe3-925c-98de701e3311" :question/math-academy-id "q-94143" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}+\\boxed{}\\,{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{1}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =(3{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} & =3([{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}]-{x}_{2}^{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} & =3({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ $2\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c4037e5-607d-5cc4-9ce8-f7f1cd2c5aae" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 2D-Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62c29d18-b7ad-5791-8c03-010ac438d1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and ${x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379084} {:answer/id #uuid "afdb44de-b78f-5507-aa8d-c70bfb219802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and ${x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379085} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe3adfe-57b7-5d0f-ad71-b8d9794a5244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379086} {:answer/id #uuid "8ddf4364-5244-5bdd-aa16-ebc15eb6ab10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379087} {:answer/id #uuid "a4dd6dc8-b571-5f12-91f8-b27a856298aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and ${x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62c29d18-b7ad-5791-8c03-010ac438d1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and ${x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379084} :answer-field/id #uuid "21ff659b-1664-5cf5-ac6b-8c2df62d69a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8717eb0-f4e1-50fd-9190-b8cfaaa41b81" :question/math-academy-id "q-94149" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+38{x}_{2}^{2}+16{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,{y}_{1}^{2}+6{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{1}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+38{x}_{2}^{2}+16{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =(2{x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+38{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+38{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+38{x}_{2}^{2} & =2([{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}]-16{x}_{2}^{2})+38{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(({x}_{1}+4{x}_{2})^{2}-16{x}_{2}^{2})+38{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(({x}_{1}+4{x}_{2})^{2}-16{x}_{2}^{2})+38{x}_{2}^{2} & =2({x}_{1}+4{x}_{2})^{2}-32{x}_{2}^{2}+38{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}+4{x}_{2})^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n2{y}_{1}^{2}+6{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ $2\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e5e149-54cd-528e-b50e-1cafecb7b77a" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 3D-Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a19f4bc2-1eff-5d61-ac6f-ba2f83c0f85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,4{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379091} {:answer/id #uuid "6798bdff-a1c2-504e-a1b9-96f3cbc58e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,2{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379092} {:answer/id #uuid "1773cc8c-d7aa-5e0e-951d-4a26990ade13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,2{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379093} {:answer/id #uuid "044e4aeb-fc06-5887-8a27-02272a62413b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379094} {:answer/id #uuid "8698ef57-424a-572d-84ec-ba767557453e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8698ef57-424a-572d-84ec-ba767557453e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379095} :answer-field/id #uuid "988a02cb-e88b-5820-b895-a5baa72b817d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8abbbd8d-56c6-5a50-8378-69e23e070d79" :question/math-academy-id "q-94159" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+18{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+18{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{3})-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+18{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}))-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+18{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-4{x}_{3})+({x}_{2}-4{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-4{x}_{3})^{2})-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+18{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-4{x}_{3})^{2}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+18{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-16{x}_{3}^{2}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+18{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, top to bottom, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e5e149-54cd-528e-b50e-1cafecb7b77a" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 3D-Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8be1381c-958f-5b99-b987-bb52f1846989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379098} {:answer/id #uuid "5cdc41f5-49de-5f52-9549-21fd321502d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,2{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379099} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc92af0-9a8e-5ea8-aca4-5c78c9252623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379100} {:answer/id #uuid "2329df49-9c72-5a21-bb26-bb180125e251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,2{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379101} {:answer/id #uuid "21ea4d25-b9d3-51f0-ad86-196cf61c82cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,5{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8be1381c-958f-5b99-b987-bb52f1846989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379098} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b3d5c46-d00a-5c1b-a149-8b13472268a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6292f301-a92e-5e13-a517-021abc27a23d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94161" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+26{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2}-10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-10{x}_{2}{x}_{3}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+26{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2}-10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-10{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3})-10{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+26{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}(5{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}))-10{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+26{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}(5{x}_{2}-{x}_{3})+(5{x}_{2}-{x}_{3})^{2}-(5{x}_{2}-{x}_{3})^{2})-10{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+26{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}-(5{x}_{2}-{x}_{3})^{2}-10{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+26{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}-25{x}_{2}^{2}+10{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}^{2}-10{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+26{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, top to bottom, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ ${x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e5e149-54cd-528e-b50e-1cafecb7b77a" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 3D-Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9fb1b5b-e38f-5219-949e-695d79d77720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379105} {:answer/id #uuid "25861e5e-a39f-5c2b-b4c7-a2dd1011be87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379106} {:answer/id #uuid "31655740-43d9-5ecb-af5b-a698bf0f1845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379107} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8713ec-b85f-52f9-9428-09c640f0d2b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379108} {:answer/id #uuid "839e541a-ffe0-509c-9cbc-294da6944c48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "839e541a-ffe0-509c-9cbc-294da6944c48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379109} :answer-field/id #uuid "63e491cd-1a78-50e4-8539-4a3c697daa2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad6201ba-6d80-5b8d-a89d-c34d502e4eae" :question/math-academy-id "q-94162" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3})+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}))+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}+{x}_{3})+({x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2})+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group ${x}_{2}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{2},$ factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{2}^{2},$ and complete the square inside the second parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3})-{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}+([{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2}]-{x}_{3}^{2})-{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{x}_{3})^{2}-2{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e5e149-54cd-528e-b50e-1cafecb7b77a" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 3D-Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3a6c54f-054a-53e4-8bcb-85f546bb68d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379112} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9459cd-b222-5323-883b-3b14adea5308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379113} {:answer/id #uuid "06a59861-c8de-534f-b615-879a840fc325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379114} {:answer/id #uuid "c752445e-f9e7-5e41-bd24-3e894263dc3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379115} {:answer/id #uuid "87e56994-33b9-5413-b9ba-b4da3cc0e961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87e56994-33b9-5413-b9ba-b4da3cc0e961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379116} :answer-field/id #uuid "1edfafcd-d14c-52c4-9ccf-93835779a290" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c34790ce-c36b-5dcb-a189-d84001cf999a" :question/math-academy-id "q-94179" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+8{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+8{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3})+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-{x}_{3}))+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-{x}_{3})+({x}_{2}-{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-{x}_{3})^{2})+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-{x}_{3})^{2}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}^{2}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+7{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group ${x}_{2}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{2},$ factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{2}^{2},$ and complete the square inside the second parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+7{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}+2({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3})+7{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}+2([{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{3}^{2}]-4{x}_{3}^{2})+7{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}+2(({x}_{2}+2{x}_{3})^{2}-4{x}_{3}^{2})+7{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}+2({x}_{2}+2{x}_{3})^{2}-8{x}_{3}^{2}+7{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})^{2}+2({x}_{2}+2{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}.$"}]}
